EXT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - DAY

Bright morning sun hits the red brick walls of a suburban school. A banner reads: WELCOME PAPER ENGINEER CHARLES VANCE!

INT. ART CLASSROOM - DAY

Inside, the walls are lined with colorful student paintings. A shelf holds clay sculptures.

At a back table sits REYANSH (10). He is a South Asian boy with hair swept neatly to one side. He wears a navy track jacket with white piping. Behind his left ear is a purple hearing aid. 

Reyansh has profound bilateral hearing loss. He is deaf. He does not hear the chatter of the kids around him. Instead, his eyes dart across the room, reading the environment.

On the table sits his totem: a folded paper fortune-teller. It is made of deep purple paper with clean, sharp creases.

Reyansh wants to win the District Art Showcase. He folds the corners of the paper with quick, precise fingers. He needs one thing to guarantee his win: the signature of Charles Vance.

MRS. GABLE (40s), the art teacher, walks past the tables. She carries a stack of drawing pads. 

Reyansh taps Mrs. Gable's arm. He holds up his purple paper fortune-teller. He points to the blank center square where a signature should go.

Mrs. Gable smiles, but her eyes are wide and anxious. Her shoulders are tense. She speaks quickly. Reyansh reads her lips.

MRS. GABLE
Not now, Reyansh. We have to get to the gym. The assembly is starting.

Reyansh points to the fortune-teller again. He signs with quick, precise hands.

REYANSH
(signing ASL)
Signature. Just one minute.

Mrs. Gable gently pats his shoulder, ignoring the sign. She points toward the door.

MRS. GABLE
Line up, please. Everyone in line!

Reyansh sighs. He slips the paper fortune-teller into his track jacket pocket. He feels the sharp edges against his side.

INT. AUDITORIUM - DAY

The school auditorium is packed. Hundreds of kids sit on wooden bleachers. 

Reyansh sits near the back. The ambient vibrations of stomping feet rumble through his sneakers. 

To his left, LEO (10) and SAM (10) watch him. Leo nudges Sam. Leo begins to flap his hands in random, jerky movements, pretending to sign. He laughs.

Sam snickers and copies the fake gestures.

Reyansh looks away. He does not let his face show any hurt. He keeps his posture straight. He has seen this before. He focuses his eyes on the stage.

On stage, PRINCIPAL HARRIS (50s) stands at a microphone. A large projection screen hangs behind her.

On the screen, an educational video plays. It has visible captions at the bottom: "CHARLES VANCE: MASTER OF 3D PAPER DESIGN."

Reyansh watches the video. The screen shows the real Charles Vance folding a massive paper dragon. The man's hands in the video are swift. His fingers have thick calluses on the tips. He folds without looking at his fingers, his eyes locked on the camera.

Principal Harris gestures to the side of the stage. 

PRINCIPAL HARRIS
(on microphone, captioned)
And now, please welcome the master of paper himself, Charles Vance!

An adult man steps onto the stage. He wears a tweed jacket and round glasses. He has a short beard. He looks like the man in the video.

This is FAKE VANCE (40s). 

The crowd of kids erupts into applause. The vibrations shake the floorboards.

Fake Vance walks to the podium. He smiles wide. He waves both hands.

Reyansh leans forward. His eyes narrow. 

He watches Fake Vance's face. 

As the man smiles, his left eyebrow twitches upward. The muscles around his eyes do not crinkle. His lips are pulled tight, showing too much teeth. 

It is a microexpression of fear. Not excitement. Fear.

Fake Vance takes a piece of green paper from the podium. He begins to speak into the microphone.

FAKE VANCE
(captioned on screen)
Thank you, students! Today, we will create magic from simple sheets of paper.

Fake Vance tries to fold the green paper. He creases the edge. His hands shake. He presses down with his palm, tearing the corner slightly.

Reyansh watches the hands. There are no calluses. The movements are clumsy, heavy, and uncertain.

Reyansh reaches into his pocket and touches his own paper fortune-teller. He knows the weight of paper. He knows the delicate pressure needed for a perfect crease. This man on stage is not an engineer.

Reyansh taps Mrs. Gable's arm again. He pulls out a small notepad and a pencil. He writes in big, clear letters: "HE IS LYING. NOT VANCE."

He shows it to Mrs. Gable.

Mrs. Gable looks at the notepad, then looks at Reyansh. She frowns, her face softening with pity. She speaks slowly, exaggerating her lip movements.

MRS. GABLE
Reyansh, he is just nervous. It is a big crowd. Keep watching.

Reyansh shakes his head. He points to his eyes, then to the stage. 

REYANSH
(signing ASL)
Look at his hands. Clumsy. Look at his eyes. He is hiding.

Mrs. Gable taps her own ear, then shakes her head. 

MRS. GABLE
Just listen to the presentation, sweetie. Shh.

Reyansh feels the familiar sting. Because he is a deaf boy, they think he does not understand the situation. They think his eyes are lying to him.

On stage, Fake Vance continues his speech.

FAKE VANCE
(captioned)
And at the end of today's assembly, we will collect the school's generous donations for the Young Artists Foundation. Five thousand dollars to help kids just like you!

Principal Harris holds up a large, oversized novelty check for $5,000. 

Fake Vance's eyes dart toward the check. His tongue quicky darts out to wet his upper lip. Another microexpression. Greed.

Reyansh looks toward the side of the stage. 

A second man in a dark gray windbreaker stands in the shadows of the wings. He holds a heavy metal briefcase. He keeps looking back toward the exit doors.

Reyansh decides he cannot wait. He needs to get closer. He needs to find the truth.

He slips off the bleachers. Mrs. Gable is busy watching the stage and does not notice him leave.

INT. BACKSTAGE CORRIDOR - DAY

The hallway behind the stage is quiet. The walls are painted a light gray. 

Reyansh walks silently on his rubber-soled sneakers. He keeps his back against the wall. 

He feels a subtle vibration through the concrete floor. A rhythmic thud. 

Thump. Thump. Thump.

It is not the beat of the music from the assembly. It is a physical impact nearby.

Reyansh follows the vibration. He stops in front of a heavy wooden door labeled: UTILITY CLOSET.

The thumping is coming from inside.

Reyansh looks around. The hallway is empty. He approaches the door. He presses his hand flat against the wood. 

He feels the vibration clearly now. Someone is kicking the door from the inside.

Reyansh reaches into his pocket. He pulls out his purple paper fortune-teller. He slides the flat, folded paper into the narrow gap beneath the door.

He lets go of one end. 

From the other side of the door, the paper is suddenly tugged. It wiggles.

Someone is in there.

Reyansh grabs the brass doorknob. It is locked. He looks up at the lock mechanism. It is a simple latch.

He looks down at his paper fortune-teller. He unfolds it carefully, turning it into a stiff, flat sheet of thick cardstock. 

He slides the edge of the stiff paper into the door gap, aiming for the latch. He pushes hard. 

The paper bends, but holds its shape. With a soft click, the latch releases.

Reyansh pushes the door open.

INT. UTILITY CLOSET - CONTINUOUS

The closet is dark, lit only by a dusty window. 

Tied to a wooden chair is a man in a rumpled tweed jacket. He has a real beard and round glasses. His mouth is covered with silver duct tape.

This is the REAL CHARLES VANCE (50s).

Real Vance's eyes are wide with panic. On his fingers, Reyansh sees the thick, yellowed calluses of a master paper engineer.

Reyansh steps inside. He does not gasp. He acts quickly. 

He reaches up and gently peels the tape off the man's mouth.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
(gasping for air)
Thank goodness! He locked me in here! He took my clothes, my notes...

Reyansh holds up a hand, stopping him. He points to his purple hearing aid, then to his own ears. He shakes his head.

Real Vance stops. He looks at Reyansh's navy jacket, then at the unfolded purple paper in Reyansh's hand.

Real Vance nods. He speaks clearly, keeping his face forward so Reyansh can read his lips.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
An impostor. He is on stage. He wants the check.

Reyansh nods. He signs quickly.

REYANSH
(signing ASL)
I know. I saw his face. He is lying.

Real Vance looks at Reyansh's hands. He smiles. He signs back, his movements slightly rusty but clear.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
(signing ASL)
Help me. Untie me.

Reyansh quickly unties the thick rope around the real Vance's arms. 

Once free, Real Vance stands up. He rubs his wrists. He looks at Reyansh's purple paper.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
That is beautiful paper. Did you fold that?

Reyansh nods. He quickly refolds the paper back into the fortune-teller shape. He holds it out.

REYANSH
(signing ASL)
Sign it? Later?

REAL CHARLES VANCE
(signing ASL)
Yes. But first, we stop him.

INT. STAGE WINGS - DAY

Reyansh and Real Vance creep to the edge of the stage. 

On stage, Principal Harris is handing the giant check to Fake Vance. The crowd is cheering.

PRINCIPAL HARRIS
(captioned)
We are so proud to present this to the Young Artists Foundation!

Fake Vance reaches for the check. His hands are greedy.

In the opposite wings, the accomplice in the gray windbreaker holds the metal briefcase open. He is ready to run.

Real Vance starts to step onto the stage, but Reyansh grabs his arm. 

Reyansh points to the accomplice in the opposite wings. If they just yell, the accomplice will run with the money already collected in the lobby.

Reyansh points to the giant green button on the wall next to them. It is labeled: MAIN CURTAIN.

Reyansh looks at Real Vance. He holds up three fingers, counting down.

Three. Two. One.

Reyansh slams his palm onto the green button.

With a loud whir, the heavy purple velvet curtains crash down.

They do not close from the sides. They drop from the ceiling.

The heavy fabric lands directly on top of Fake Vance, trapping him like a tent. He yells, his voice muffled by the thick velvet.

FAKE VANCE
(from under curtain)
Hey! What is happening? Let me out!

At the same time, Real Vance runs across the backstage corridor and blocks the exit door just as the accomplice tries to escape. 

The accomplice trips over a stack of folded chairs. The metal briefcase flies open, spilling pamphlets, but no money.

Principal Harris and Mrs. Gable run into the wings. They are gasping.

MRS. GABLE
What is going on here?

Real Vance steps forward, holding the accomplice by his jacket sleeve.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
This man locked me in the closet! He is a fraud. The man under the curtain is his partner.

Principal Harris looks at Real Vance, then at the curtain where Fake Vance is struggling.

Mrs. Gable looks at Reyansh. 

Reyansh stands by the curtain control panel. He pulls his purple paper fortune-teller from his pocket. He holds it up, his face calm and steady.

Mrs. Gable's face turns red with realization. She remembers the notepad.

MRS. GABLE
Reyansh... you knew. You tried to tell me.

Reyansh nods. He does not gloat. He simply signs.

REYANSH
(signing ASL)
I saw his face. His eyes. He did not know paper.

INT. AUDITORIUM - LATER

The police have led the two scammers away in handcuffs.

The students are still in the bleachers, talking excitedly.

On stage, the curtains are open again. The real Charles Vance stands at the microphone. He has his proper presentation slides on the screen. The captions display his words perfectly.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
(captioned)
Paper engineering is about precision. It is about seeing the details that others miss. Today, a student here showed me what true precision looks like.

Real Vance looks toward the back of the room.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
(captioned)
Reyansh, please come up.

The crowd turns. 

Leo and Sam stop talking. They look at Reyansh with wide eyes. Leo slowly lowers his hands, looking ashamed.

Reyansh walks down the center aisle. He does not run. He walks with a steady, confident stride. His navy track jacket is zipped up. His purple hearing aid catches the stage light.

He reaches the stage. 

Real Vance does not try to speak for him. He hands Reyansh a silver marker.

Reyansh pulls out his purple paper fortune-teller. 

Real Vance takes the marker and signs his name in clean, elegant script right in the center square of the fortune-teller.

REAL CHARLES VANCE
(signing ASL)
To a master observer.

Reyansh smiles. He signs back.

REYANSH
(signing ASL)
Thank you.

Reyansh turns to the crowd. He holds up his signed paper fortune-teller. 

The auditorium erupts into applause. Reyansh cannot hear the sound, but he feels the deep, warm vibrations of the clapping hands through the stage floor. 

He looks at the front row. Mrs. Gable is signing "clapping" with her hands high in the air, a giant smile on her face.

Reyansh slips the signed totem back into his pocket. He is a deaf boy, and he has everything he needs to win the showcase.

FADE OUT.